  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 401 PM EDT FRI OCT 9 2026\n\nOur primary feature starting the long term period will continue to \nbe the remnant low from Hurricane Isaias. The stacked low is \nexpected to be over or near central KY around dawn on Sunday. By \nthat time, drier air will be wrapping around and into the system's \nweakening circulation, with latest guidance suggesting this drier \nslot passing northeast over the JKL forecast area Sunday morning. \nEventual details of timing, track and strength of the remnant low \nare not locked in, and that will determine where and when features \naffect us. That being the case, the POP remains elevated through the \nday on Sunday (even through there may very well be a break in precip \nfor a time). Have tried to hold the highest (categorical) POP with \nthe greater moisture still surrounding the remaining core of the \nsystem as it passes through around mid day and afternoon on Sunday. \nThe strongest winds should be finished by Sunday, and it looks like \nthere's a lack of deep instability. So, the character of the day \nappears to be showery with limited warming.\n\nThe circulation should be departing to our northeast by Sunday \nevening, with the back edge of the precip forecast to be over the \neastern portion of the JKL forecast area by then. It will probably \nend during the night, but a few light showers can't be ruled out yet \non Monday morning over the eastern tip of the state. Transient \nridging then passes over from west to east during the day Monday, \nwith warm air advection becoming pronounced once the low level \nridging slips past by late in the day. Low level moisture (with \nsurface dew points in the upper 50s/lower 60s) is expected to linger \nin the wake of the tropical system, but very dry air aloft should \nmake a comeback with the arrival of the mid-upper level ridging. \nThis keeps us mild and dry at least into the day Tuesday. \n\nAfter this point, the forecast becomes more uncertain as model \ndiscrepancies come into the picture. The uncertainties initially \ninvolve a cold front moving into the Ohio Valley from the northwest \nby late Tuesday, and how much southeastward progress this front \neventually makes. Other issues arise with model differences in the \ntiming/positioning of a strong upper trough expected to develop \nsoutheastward across the Great Lakes and northeast CONUS at the end \nof the week, and its influence on its associated cold front. The \nmodel blend represented in the forecast is very much subject to \nchange with time.",
